Two names, and both are the COMDAT difference every other .nocl in this directory records: `??0S@@QEAA@UT@@@Z` and `??0T@@QEAA@XZ`, the constructors of the two classes the most vexing parse is written with. Nothing calls them - `S vexing(T());` is a *function declaration*, which is the point of that line - and cl emits a constructor defined inside its class into a COMDAT the collector does not read, where cxx1 has no weak or linkonce linkage and emits an ordinary definition. `?vexing@@YA?AUS@@P6A?AUT@@XZ@Z` itself agrees, which is the name this case wanted from the Windows box: cl reads the declaration as a function and so does cxx1.

Every name this case is *about* agrees too, and there are few of them by construction: `int z(5);` is a store into a frame slot and produces no symbol at all. `?global@@3HA`, `?scaled@@3NB` and the four declared functions match cl exactly.
